{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 160,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First we discovered Earth's liquid core. \nThen we discovered a solid core within"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5040,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that liquid core. Then we discovered that \nthe solid core has bizarre properties that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9360,
    "dDurationMs": 12560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are more liquid like. So is the inner \ncore solid or liquid? The answer is yes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21920,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get started, a couple quick \nannouncements. First, we have some new data:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27120,
    "dDurationMs": 9680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "liking and commenting really does help get \nepisodes shared. And we’ve also learned that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the number one reason people support us on \nPatreon isn’t actually the perks—it’s simply to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42720,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "support the Space Time community and the work we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45200,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do. So for those of you who do \nsupport thank you so so much."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But don't get me wrong, there are perks and \nthey're good. There's a link in the description"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56640,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you'd like to join, this \nreally would be a huge help."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60960,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Next up. Today’s episode is part of \nPBS Earth Month! PBS is celebrating by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66080,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "releasing a ton of great new episodes diving \ndeep into our amazing planet. In particular,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71520,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you might enjoy the latest episode of \neons, which covers super mountains,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76160,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these ridiculously enormous formations that, as \nfar as we know, have only ever existed on Earth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "twice. Links to that episode and the full Earth \nMonth playlist also in the description. Next up,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86800,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we're excited to launch our universe \nin a Black Hole T-shirt and hoodie. If"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90000,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you believe we're trapped inside a black \nhole where space and time swapped roles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93440,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the Big Bang was just the other side of \na collapse, then this is the shirt for you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97040,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We're also still offering up \nour black hole light curves,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "desktop and gaming mat that shows the ways light \nis warped by the gravity of a black hole. Plus,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104720,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our UV glow kit, rotating black hole designs, \nand dark energy mugs are all still available."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109920,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Links in the description. Now onto the \nepisode. And no. It may be that Earth's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115360,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inner core is in an exotic state of matter \nthat is simultaneously crystalline and fluid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The super ionic state the mysteries of \nour own planet's interior have in many"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128320,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ways been harder to crack than \nthose of the rest of the cosmos."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133200,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, we can send probes to the edge of the \nsolar system and the 46 billion light years to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the cosmic horizon, a largely transparent. \nAnd we can see the most distant galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144320,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the 6400 km to Earth's center are both"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148880,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "opaque to light and far beyond the \nreach of any conventional drill."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153120,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The best we can do is to listen to the \nfaint rumblings of distant earthquakes,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then try to piece together how those seismic \nwaves bounce around Earth's interior. To give"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164880,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you an idea of how seismology has lagged \ncosmology and the rest of physics,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171280,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it wasn't until 1909, after Einstein, special \nrelativity, that anyone realized that seismic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177840,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "waves could teach us about Earth's structure. It \nwas Andrija Mohorovičić that noticed that some"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184240,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the P waves that's primary or pressure waves, \nfound a shortcut from the earthquake's epicenter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192800,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to seismic stations hundreds of kilometers away. \nHe brilliantly inferred that the waves traveled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199520,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through a denser and hence higher velocity region \ndeeper in the Earth, thereby discovering the crust"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206400,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mantle boundary. That opened the floodgates. \nIn 1914, just a year before general relativity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the following prediction of black holes, \nwe discovered Earth's molten core. That was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219040,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by Benno Gutenberg, who found that S-waves, \nfor secondary or shear, were blocked across"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a broad shadow region on the opposite \nside of the planet to their origin while"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230400,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the P-waves could make it straight through. \nShear waves only propagate through solids."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236560,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So he realized that this meant there \nmust be a liquid interior to the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242480,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even those P waves play funny around the \nmolten core, refracting to create smaller shadow"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249520,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "zones. Taking this game one level deeper. It was \nin 1936, long after we discovered the expansion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257280,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the universe, and inferred the Big Bang \nthat Inge Lehmann discovered the inner solid"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263600,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "core. She found that some P waves did enter \ntheir shadow zones, and brilliantly figured"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270240,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out that these must be reflecting off a solid \nregion even deeper than the molten interior."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276880,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, better late than never. These ideas \nhave been refined and verified over the years,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283040,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leading to our modern, multi-layered model \nof Earth's interior. Our planet is an"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289040,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "oblate spheroid with a low density crust that \ncarries slow P-waves and even slower S-waves,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295600,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "followed by a denser, higher \nwave speed rocky mantle that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slowly flows even though it's still solid, \nallowing it to support both wave types."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306160,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then we have the liquid outer \ncore breathing a solid inner core,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both mostly of iron. It all hangs together \nneatly, explaining our dynamic planet,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from its volcanism to its tectonics to its \nmagnetic field. For all intents and purposes,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323200,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's right. So what do we do when new, \neven more refined seismic data disagrees"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with this model? As our seismic monitoring \nbecame more sensitive and more widespread,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we started to probe the inner structure in more \ndetail, and we started to see some glitches."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, P-waves pass through \nthe inner core faster in the polar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "direction than the equatorial direction. \nThis is typically explained by the fact"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351440,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that sound speed in a crystal can depend on the \norientation relative to the crystal's lattice."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358160,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If Earth's inner core is crystalline iron \nand the lattice orientation correlates"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with planetary spin, then the polar \nequatorial difference can be explained."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370160,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There even seems to be an east west \nhemispheric asymmetry in wave speed,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that's taken as evidence of large scale \nlumpiness and or melt regions in the inner core."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380640,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the hardest things to explain is really \ndeep in the weeds of the data, but it may reveal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387680,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "something really startling about the heart \nof our planet. S-waves, which are completely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393840,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "blocked by the molten outer core, do arise in \nthe inner core through conversion of P-waves,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400560,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it's actually wild that we can even identify \nsurface seismic waves that have gone through a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407920,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "P-S, then S-P transition through Earth's core, \nthen back to P-waves to return to the surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we do find that these core shear \nwaves travel way too slowly in that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421440,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "core compared to what we expect, \ngiven the core's composition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425600,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "S-waves in the core are also losing energy \nmuch more quickly as they travel than is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432400,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expected for a stiff material like crystalline \niron. Now, this inner core S-wave thing is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a real current mystery, and to figure it \nout, we're going to need some geophysics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444400,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are two main ways to deform a solid \ncompression/expansion. So changing the volume. And"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451120,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that's what P-waves do. Or shearing changing the \nshape. And that's our S-waves. The more resistant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458000,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a material is to either of these, the faster that \nmaterial will propagate waves in that mode. Think"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464320,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about it as a more easily deformable material, \nbeing sort of soft and sludgy in that mode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470480,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Crystalline iron is very stiff to both compression \nand shear, and so should have fast P and S waves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And Earth's core does transmit P waves as fast \nas we expect. But the S waves are too slow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484640,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To put numbers on this. The relative \nshear-ability to compressibility of a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489280,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "material is encapsulated by the Poisson's \nratio, which is usually around 0.2 or 0.3"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496720,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for a typical solid. But Earth's \ncore seems to be at around 0.45,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is close to the Poisson's ratio \nfor rubber. So not very compressible,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507840,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but very shareable. Let's call such a \nmaterial squidgy. That's not a technical term,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515040,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so don't use it on any geophysicists. So what \nsort of core material could be squidgy like this?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523840,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well we're going to stick with iron in general \nbecause we know that this matches the density."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529520,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And because we see that asteroids do develop \nthese iron cores as the heavy metal drips to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535520,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the center during cooling. But there are various"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537920,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things that could increase \nthe cores's squidginess."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541520,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first idea people had was to \njust add other stuff to the crystal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546080,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lattice. Alloying iron crystal \nwith hydrogen, carbon, oxygen,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550720,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "silicon sulfur will significantly increase \nthe poisson's ratio and so lower S-wave speed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557440,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it's now generally believed that all by \nitself, just alloying with any reasonable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amount of these light elements could not \nreduce S-wave speed by enough. Another"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568000,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possibility is to make the core grainy. Rather \nthan one giant spherical crystal, the lattice"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the core could be fragmented, potentially on \nmicroscopic scales. When molten metal solidifies,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580560,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its crystal lattice grows from multiple nucleation \npoints, resulting in many misaligned sectors."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587280,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over time, these grains can merge and align to \nproduce larger lattices or even fragment further,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593920,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it's not clear what we should \nexpect to have happened in Earth's core."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598320,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because the boundaries between grains \nlacked the strength of the pure lattice."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603120,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The more able to slide against each \nother, and this is greatly amplified"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608080,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if microscopic membranes of molten or heat \nsoftened metal formed between those grains."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615040,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This granular ization and melts or pre-built \ncan potentially explain the high Poisson's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ratio in the core. But the challenge with \nthis explanation is that there's only a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "narrow window of grain size, plus melt or \npre-melt that gives us the right core properties"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631440,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to explain S-wave propagation, push it too \nfar and the core goes from squidgy to goopy,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638400,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meaning that's waves lose way too much \nenergy compared to what we observe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644160,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's also the issue that fine grained crystal \nstructures have a harder time organizing globally."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And remember, we need some level of global \nlattice alignment to explain the polar versus"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equatorial speed difference. Most likely \nthere is some of these granular ization,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "etc. going on, but there are strong arguments \nthat they aren't the whole explanation. We're"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666800,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "looking for a way to retain the general rigidity \nof the core, potentially allowing a global crystal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672800,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lattice alignment, but to still lubricate \nthings a bit to slow down them S-waves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What we need is a new state of matter, a \nstate of matter that allows the core to be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684400,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simultaneously solid and liquid. And so let me \nintroduce the superionic state. In this state,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692320,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we have a rigid crystal lattice of some element \nto a molecule with some other atom moving"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "freely within this structure. There are lots of \nexamples of superionic materials with various"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technological applications, from batteries \nand fuel cells to various types of sensor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708960,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We've even observed superionic ice in which \nhydrogen moves freely within an oxygen lattice."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of Earth's core, the primary \nlattice would be the hexagonal lattice,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formed by iron alloyed with nickel. \nLighter elements like hydrogen, oxygen,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and most notably carbon, could then live \nin the interstitial spaces of that lattice,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732160,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moving with a freedom that is liquid like."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734720,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, iron doesn't do this in normal \ncircumstances. At low temperature,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738480,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impurities like carbon tend to stay \nat fixed positions in the lattice,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but molecular dynamic simulations have \nshown that as temperatures increase,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "carbon atoms begin to move between interstitial \nsites and above a certain temperature, they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755360,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exhibit liquid like behavior. According to these \nsimulations, the high pressure in a core of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761760,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth creates the perfect conditions for the iron \ncarbon lattice to enter this superionic state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770160,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even better, in these simulations, the alloy \nshows a lower shear velocity than pure iron,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777440,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and a Poisson's ratio of around 0.43, very \nclose to the observed seismic properties"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the real inner core. Simulations are \ngreat because they can suggest and sharpen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788480,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hypotheses that we can then go and test in \nthe real world. Now it's going to be a long,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793200,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "long time before we can access \nEarth's core to do direct tests."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797600,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So in the meantime, we need to recreate \nthe conditions of the core in our labs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802560,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is where the new study by Huang, \nHe, Zhang et al comes in. This team created"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808720,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a hexagonal close packed iron lattice \nwith a small amount of carbon dissolved"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interstitially. Then they smacked it with \nparticles traveling at high speed to create"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818480,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shock pressures and corresponding temperatures \nhigh enough to unlock the super ionic state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824400,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, the theme of this episode seems to be sound \nspeed in different materials. So one side note"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the projectile in this experiment \nwas accelerated by a light gas gun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835760,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The projectile velocity in a firearm is \nconstrained by the speed of sound. Just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840960,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because a bullet is pushed out \nof the nozzle by expanding gas,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845120,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that expansion is limited by sound speed, \nbut sound speed increases with molecular mess."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For air, that's basically dinitrogen. If \ninstead you fill the chamber with hydrogen or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858000,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "helium, then a higher sound speed behind the \nbullet results in a faster muzzle velocity,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864560,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where normal firearms can achieve \nmuzzle velocities of Mach a few. A"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869920,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light gas projectile can hit Mach 20 plus or \nactual orbital velocities. Because of that,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877200,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they're often used to study the impacts of, \nsay, micro meteoroids on space based hardware."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883520,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in this experiment, the light gas gun is \ngiving us a sort of particle accelerator for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888960,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "geology. Anyway, ends tangent mode. And let's \nget back to the experiment. Once the supersonic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895120,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "state was created in the iron lattice, its \nproperties were studied similar to how we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899680,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "study Earth's interior by looking at vibrations \non the surface. In this case, with photon Doppler"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "velocimetry, which is basically pointing \na super precise speed gun at the surface,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910800,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now those vibrations reveal the structure of the \ninterior of the sample, just as with seismology."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this way, the team could infer various \nproperties, including the shear velocity and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923360,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Poisson's ratio, and these values were \nconsistent with what we expect from super"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929760,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ionic iron. As predicted by simulations. It's \nworth mentioning that this experiment didn't"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "achieve the full pressure and temperature of \nEarth's inner core, but at the very least,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we seem to have produced this strange \nphase of matter in an iron carbon alloy,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946160,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is an experimental validation of a mechanism \nthat had previously existed only in simulation,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953760,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the supersonic state produced exhibits strong \nshear softening consistent with seismic data that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961600,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strengthens its viability as a candidate for \nthe unusual \"squidginess\" of Earth's inner core."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the superironic hypothesis turned out to \nbe true, it may help explain other things."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972880,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, preferential flow of \nthe interstitial carbon along Earth's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rotational axis could partially account \nfor the polar equatorial speed difference,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982000,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which loosens the constraints on global \nlattice alignment, and the flow of this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987600,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "carbon may even participate in the geo-dynamo \neffect that powers Earth's magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992560,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we should also consider this a victory \nfor the philosophy at the heart of all of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997280,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics that the secrets of nature are best \nrevealed by smashing things to other things"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004000,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really hard. Earth's interior below us \nis in many ways harder to explore than"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the vast cosmos above. Fortunately, \nthe secrets of both are encoded in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1015520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vibrations that reach the narrow sliver \nof our human world from both directions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020800,
    "dDurationMs": 24912,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so we build our maps of both \nastrophysical and geophysical space time."
    } ]
  } ]
}
